.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd September 25, 2026
.Dt EMERGENCY 8
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm emergency
.Nd repair a system whose init keeps dying
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm /sbin/emergency
.Sh DESCRIPTION
When process 1 ends, the kernel records why and starts it again.
If it ends three times within 30 seconds, the kernel stops restarting it
and runs
.Nm
as process 1 instead.
.Pp
.Nm
lists each recorded end of process 1 \(em its time, and its exit status or
the signal that killed it, with the faulting address \(em and offers two
choices: a root shell to repair the system, or restarting the computer.
When the shell exits,
.Nm
exits too, and the kernel starts
.Xr init 8
again, in recovery mode.
.Pp
If the console is marked
.Cm insecure
in
.Xr ttys 5 ,
the shell requires root's password, unless root has none.
.Pp
The kernel runs
.Nm
from a copy built into the kernel, so it works even if
.Pa /sbin
is damaged.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /proc/initdeaths -compact
.It Pa /proc/initdeaths
the kernel's record of process 1's ends, one per line:
.Ar seconds Cm exit Ar status ,
or
.Ar seconds Cm signal Ar number
.Op Cm ip Ar address Op Cm addr Ar address
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ttys 5 ,
.Xr init 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
program first appeared in OxideBSD.
Other BSDs halt the system when process 1 exits.
